they've lost daughters and sons a year  after the collapse of Rana Plaza they're  still mourning the dead and demanding  compensation from the international  companies whose contractors made clothes  there a memorial for the most  devastating accident in the history of  the garment industry and of a system of  exploitation when you come here you  immediately feel a deep sadness you see  things on the ground like clothes and  bits of fabric and things that remind  you of everyday life in the factory he's  really upset  Martinez Schmidt works with the GI Z in  Bangladesh he runs a project aimed at  improving conditions in the factories  those can turn everyone even the clothes  I'm wearing right now come from  Bangladesh people give their lives here  to make clothes for Europeans it's  pretty shocking this show kid I'm not  sure these in Bangladesh there are more  than 5,000 clothing factories employing  four million people in this industry the  pressure is immense  after Rana Plaza few factories are  prepared to open their doors to  journalists but we are allowed to film  here a model factory where Jonna to  effect do she works before this I never  worked anywhere for long I couldn't take  the other factories for more than five  or six months but here it's okay  I've been here seven years the firm's  that order clothes from here are mostly  European they include CNA it has sourced  clothes from this factory for years  working with GI Z and Magnus Schmidt  sadly safety standards are still bad in  many factories for example the firewalls  are inadequate the stairways don't have  fireproof doors and teams aren't trained  to respond if a fire breaks out that's  what's missing from a lot of factories  Schmidt and the C&amp;A Foundation are  trying to improve product and safety  standards in the factories the  foundation's pilot project is running in  five of the firm's 150 factories in  Bangladesh so is the project just a PR  exercise if it was just for the image we  wouldn't be continuing to be testing it  you know and we're also sharing the  lessons actually to the entire industry  so right now via in the middle of  publishing a case study about the  program because I think we can learn a  lot before we scale up the program but  other brands can learn as well the idea  of suddenly introducing European  production standards isn't popular with  many factory owners in Bangladesh but  owner Fuad abdullah sees it as an  opportunity still the biggest problem he  faces is buyer's pressure to keep prices  low international companies come to  Bangladesh because they know that wages  are low here if you ask me that way I  don't think there's a fairness but  that's how the world is budget for  clothing is the least for people there  will be money for a mobile phone there  will be money for holidays but everyone  look for $10 jeans and it's driven by  the market demand it's not the buyers I  don't trying to sell it cheap in  Bangladesh the minimum wage for garment  workers has just risen to 50 euros a  month  with lots of overtime seamstress John  Atul fig do she can make 85 euros per  month but in Dhaka rents rise with  garment workers wages and like many  '[ __ ] do she also has a family to  support of course I miss my family I  live here by myself my father my mother  they all live far away in the country  even when things aren't going so well  I'm still the only one who's earning so  I sent home as much as I can 1,100  people died at Rana Plaza 2,000 more  were injured some severely many victims  of the accident are still struggling to  recover  Reihana Akhtar was forced to go to work  on the day of the accident though there  was already a gaping hole in the  building a wall and then the ceiling  collapsed on her she lost her leg Rehana  has received some money from the  government but none from the clothing  companies is my leg really only worth  9,000 euros for the rest of my life I've  lost the chance to earn good money and I  can't he